    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with a heavy focus on exploiting favorable matchups against secondary rotation arms, headlined by the trio of **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (74.56), **Josh Lowe** (72.21), and **Kyle Tucker** (70.95). **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** is our standout play; he benefits immensely from an elite +19% SP skill adjustment against Young, bolstered by a +14% platoon edge. Similarly, **Josh Lowe** and **Nolan Schanuel** (69.91) are high-ceiling options against Sasaki; while the SP xERA of 5.23 is enticing, the addition of favorable wind at the park provides a critical boost to their power projections. **Drake Baldwin** also earns a prime spot in the lineup, as his leadoff role combined with a generous matchup against Bello provides high-floor run production.\n\nStrategic decision-making on the bench was centered on **Coby Mayo** (57.80), who narrowly missed the final cut. Despite his strong platoon outlook against Lovelady, the sheer density of higher-efficiency options like **Andy Pages** (63.02) and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (63.48) forced a conservative rotation. **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** remains a mandatory start despite a slight -5% penalty due to wind blowing in, as his raw power profile against Flaherty remains too valuable to ignore. We are also closely monitoring **Jackson Merrill** (56.83); while currently listed as a \"Lineup Pending\" play against the formidable Kirby, his dynamic platoon advantage keeps his floor viable, though his score is suppressed by the elite skill set of the opposing starter.\n\nFinal roster considerations remain fluid regarding **Sam Antonacci** (57.27) and **Liam Hicks** (45.47), both of whom occupy lower-tier efficiency slots today. **Liam Hicks** faces a stiff challenge against Rasmussen, and while his platoon advantage is positive, his low efficiency score suggests we should remain vigilant regarding his game cap consumption. We are maintaining depth on the bench with **Miguel Vargas**, **Josh Jung**, and **Dominic Smith**; their scores hovered in the 51-56 range, confirming that while they provide coverage, they currently lack the situational alignment required to displace our primary starters.",
